<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # School Life Adventures – Lesson # 3</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 3</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Speaking</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 2nd Grade (CEFR: Pre-A1.4)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> School Life Adventures</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> That's a Big Book!</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Speaking: Productive: Contribute to storytelling sessions with simple sentences based on visual prompts. Interactive: Use greetings and farewells appropriately in conversations with peers.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Speaking: Can contribute simple sentences to storytelling sessions using visual prompts (e.g., "The book is big." when shown a picture of a large book). Can use appropriate greetings and farewells in brief peer interactions (e.g., "How are you?" "Bye," and "See you tomorrow."). Can ask and respond in a predictable pattern to questions about familiar objects (e.g., responding to "What color is the bag?" with "It's brown.").</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="background-color: #f2f2f2;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 34%; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task (Engagement, Modeling and Clarification)</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher greets students, "Good morning, everyone! How are you today?" and encourages choral responses. Then, the teacher leads a "Classroom Object Simon Says" activity using realia. For example, "Simon says, 'Point to a big book!'" "Simon says, 'Touch a red pencil!'" "Simon says, 'Find a small bag!'" This activates previously learned vocabulary (book, pencil, bag, big, small, red, blue, green, yellow) and introduces the sounds /b/ in "book" and /s/ in "small" through repetition. Students practice polite greetings and responding to instructions.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher holds up a flashcard of a classroom object (e.g., a big, blue book). "Look! This is a big, blue book." The teacher points to the book, emphasizes "big" and "blue," and clearly pronounces /b/ in "book". Then, the teacher holds up a small, red pencil. "This is a small, red pencil." The teacher emphasizes "small" and "red," highlighting /s/ in "small" and /p/ in "pencil." The teacher models asking, "What color is the book?" and answers, "It's blue."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher uses simple questions and gestures to check understanding.
                <ul style="margin-top: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Holding a big book and a small book) "Is this book big or small?" (Gesture for big/small).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Pointing to a blue bag) "What color is this bag? Red or blue?" (Teacher prompts 'Blue!').</li>
                </ul></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 minutes</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "School Life Adventures".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> Illustrated classroom poster showing various objects (book, bag, pencil, desk, chair, eraser, ruler, board, marker, crayon, paper, shelf, folder) with varying sizes and colors, engaging with the theme "That's a Big Book!"</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> The student will be able to describe objects with detail: "This is a small bag." "That's a big, blue book." and respond to questions: "What color is the book?" "It’s blue."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b> The teacher points to objects on the poster and asks open-ended questions that encourage single-word or short-phrase responses, using demonstratives "this" and "that" and WH-questions:
                <ul style="margin-top: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What is this?" (pointing to a book). Students respond chorally "A book!"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What color is that desk?" Students respond "Brown!"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Is this a big book or a small book?" Students respond "Big book!"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher then models complete sentences and has students repeat: "It's a big book." "It's a brown desk."</li>
                </ul></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">8-10 minutes</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Speaking.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students will practice describing classroom objects using adjectives (big, small, red, blue, green, yellow, brown, long, short) and the present simple ("It is a..."). They will also practice asking and responding to "What color/size is it?" and using polite expressions for asking for objects.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul style="margin-top: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice - "Describe the Object" Chain:</b> The teacher shows an object (e.g., a green marker). Teacher says, "This is a green marker." Students repeat chorally. Then, the teacher passes it to the first student, who repeats and passes it on. Each student says, "This is a green marker." (Teacher corrects pronunciation, especially /m/ in "marker" if needed). The activity is repeated with different objects and adjectives (e.g., "This is a big red book," "This is a small yellow pencil," "This is a long ruler," "This is a brown chair").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-Controlled Practice - "Color & Size Guessing Game":</b> The teacher places several flashcards of classroom objects (book, bag, desk, chair, eraser, ruler, crayon) of various colors and sizes face down on a table. In pairs, students take turns picking a card without showing it to their partner. The partner asks, "What color is it?" and "Is it big or small?" The student with the card answers (e.g., "It's blue." "It's small."). They then guess the object. Teacher circulates, prompting with "What's that?" or "Say 'It's a...'." and reminds them to use polite expressions like "Can you please tell me the color?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Pair Interaction - "My Classroom, Your Classroom":</b> Students work in pairs. Each pair gets a picture of a classroom with several labeled objects. Student A describes an object in their picture to Student B, for example, "I see a big, blue book on the shelf." "And I see a small, red bag under the desk." Student B listens and points to the object in their own picture if it matches or asks, "Where is the big, blue book?" This encourages using prepositions (on, in, under, next to, by) and the discourse marker "and" in simple sentences. The teacher ensures students take turns speaking and listening.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">15-18 minutes</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "School Life Adventures".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Collaborative Storytelling with Visual Prompts (Role-play)</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure: "Classroom Adventure Story"</b></p>
                <ul style="margin-top: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students work in small groups of 3-4. Each group receives 3-4 actual classroom objects or large flashcards (e.g., a big book, a small pencil, a red bag, a yellow crayon).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher explains that they will tell a short, simple story about their objects. Each student in the group contributes one sentence describing an object, its color, and size, or its location.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Example contribution sequence:</b>
                        <ul style="margin-top: 4px; padding-left: 20px; list-style-type: circle;">
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 2px; text-align: justify;">Student 1: (Holds up a big book) "This is a big book. It is brown."</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 2px; text-align: justify;">Student 2: (Holds up a small pencil) "And this is a small pencil. It is yellow."</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 2px; text-align: justify;">Student 3: (Places the bag on a desk) "That's a red bag. It's on the desk."</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 2px; text-align: justify;">Student 4: (Points to the objects) "The book, the pencil, and the bag are on the table."</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Before starting, students practice polite greetings and farewells within their groups: "Hello, [name]! How are you?" "I'm good, thank you. Bye, see you tomorrow!" after their mini-story.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Each group then presents their short "story" to another group or to the whole class. The focus is on speaking confidently and collaboratively.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10 minutes</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist during the "Classroom Adventure Story" activity.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students contribute simple sentences to their group's story and interact with peers using greetings and farewells.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Teacher Checklist):</b>
                <ul style="margin-top: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Intelligible pronunciation:</b> (e.g., /b/ in "book", /s/ in "small", /p/ in "pencil")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Use of target vocabulary:</b> (e.g., book, bag, pencil, desk, big, small, red, blue, green, yellow, on, in)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Clarity and simplicity of sentences:</b> (e.g., "This is a big book." "It's brown.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Collaborative interaction:</b> (e.g., taking turns, listening, using "and")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Appropriate use of greetings and farewells:</b> ("How are you?" "Bye," "See you tomorrow.")</li>
                </ul></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students draw their favorite classroom object and write one sentence about it (e.g., "It is a red book."). If writing is difficult, they can just say it to the teacher before leaving, using the sentence frame "It is a [color] [object]."</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5 minutes</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Reflection Prompts:</b>
                <ul style="margin-top: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What new words did you use to talk about classroom things today?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What was fun to talk about with your friends?</li>
                </ul></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher Reflection:</b>
                <ul style="margin-top: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well in getting students to speak? The collaborative storytelling encouraged participation and sentence building. The use of realia was very effective.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments or scaffolds are needed for the next lesson? Some students struggled to form full sentences independently; more sentence frames or choral drilling might be beneficial. Need to ensure all students are actively participating in peer interactions.</li>
                </ul></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> In Lesson 4 (Writing), students will use the vocabulary and sentence structures practiced today to write simple descriptions of classroom objects, reinforcing their ability to describe using adjectives and present simple sentences in written form.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">2-3 minutes</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>